1 17592186045417 false] [17592186082936 1074 "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{3}{2x} &= \\dfrac 9 8 \\[5pt]\n\t\t3 \\cdot 8 &= 2x \\cdot 9 \\[5pt]\n\t\t24 &= 18x\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t24 &= 18x \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{24}{18} &= \\dfrac{18x}{18} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{4}{3} &= x\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince  $x = \\dfrac{4}{3}$ is different from $x=0,$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=\\dfrac{4}{3}.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186082937 1071 "Solve $-\\dfrac{2}{5y} = \\dfrac{1}{10}$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186082937 1071 "Solve $-\\dfrac{2}{5y} = \\dfrac{1}{10}.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186082937 1074 "First, note that $y=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -\\dfrac{2}{5y}&=\\dfrac{1}{10}\\ \\dfrac{-2}{5y}&=\\dfrac{1}{10} \\\\ (-2) \\cdot 10 &= 5y \\cdot 1 \\\\ -20 &= 5y \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} -20 &= 5y \\ \\dfrac{-20}{5} &= y \\\\ -4 &= y \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y = -4$ is different from $y=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Therefore, the solution is $y=-4$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186082937 1074 "First, note that $y=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t-\\dfrac{2}{5y}&=\\dfrac{1}{10}\\\n\t\t\\dfrac{-2}{5y}&=\\dfrac{1}{10}\\\n(-2) \\cdot 10 &= 5y \\cdot 1 \\\n-20 &= 5y\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n-20 &= 5y \\\n\\dfrac{-20}{5} &= y \\\n-4 &= y\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince  $y = -4$ is different from $y=0,$ we can accept it as a solution. Therefore, the solution is $y=-4.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186082938 1071 "Solve $\\dfrac{3x}{2x+1} = \\dfrac 9 8$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186082938 1071 "Solve $\\dfrac{3x}{2x+1} = \\dfrac 9 8.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186082938 1074 "First, note that the denominator $2x+1$ cannot be zero, since division by $0$ is undefined. Therefore we cannot have\n\n$$\n2x+1=0\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ncannot be a solution.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{3x}{2x+1} &= \\dfrac 9 8 \\\\[5pt] 3x \\cdot 8 &= (2x+1) \\cdot 9 \\\\[5pt] 24x &= 18x + 9 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 24x &= 18x + 9 \\\\[5pt] 24x-18x &= 18x + 9 -18x \\\\[5pt] 6x& = 9 \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{6x}{6} & = \\dfrac{9}{6} \\\\[5pt] x &= \\dfrac{3}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nis different from\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186082938 1074 "First, note that the denominator $2x+1$ cannot be zero, since division by $0$ is undefined. Therefore we cannot have $2x+1=0,$ which means $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ cannot be a solution.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{3x}{2x+1} &= \\dfrac 9 8 \\[5pt]\n\t\t3x \\cdot 8 &= (2x+1) \\cdot 9 \\[5pt]\n\t\t24x &= 18x + 9 \n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t24x &= 18x + 9 \\[5pt]\n\t\t24x-18x &= 18x + 9 -18x \\[5pt]\n\t\t6x& = 9 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{6x}{6} & = \\dfrac{9}{6} \\[5pt]\n\t\tx &= \\dfrac{3}{2}\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince  $x = \\dfrac{3}{2}$ is different from $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186083192 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492019 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186083192 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492067 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186083193 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492034 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186083194 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491997 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186083407 1050 420.0 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186083619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186083619 1071 "The solution to $-\\frac{4}{3} = \\frac{2}{6t}$ is$t =$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186083619 1071 "The solution to $-\\frac{4}{3}=\\frac{2}{6t}$ is$\\,\\,t\\,=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186083619 1074 "First, note that $t=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction and then apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3} & =\\frac{2}{6t} \\\\ \\frac{-4}{3} & =\\frac{2}{6t} \\\\ (-4)\\cdot 6t & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\ -24t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24t & =6 \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{-24} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is different from $t=0,$ we can accept it as a solution. Therefore, the solution is $t=-\\frac{1}{4}.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186083774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186083774 1071 "Solve for $s$ where $\\frac{2s + 4}{2s + 6} = \\frac{6}{5}$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186083774 1071 "Solve for $s$ where $\\frac{2s+4}{2s+6}=\\frac{6}{5}.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186083774 1074 "First, note that the denominator $2s+6$ cannot be zero, since division by $0$ is undefined. Therefore we cannot have $2s+6=0,$ which means $s=-3$ cannot be a solution.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2s+4}{2s+6} & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ (2s+4)\\cdot 5 & =(2s+6)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 10s+20 & =12s+36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10s+20 & =12s+36 \\\\ 10s+20-10s & =12s+36-10s \\\\ 20 & =2s+36 \\\\ 20-36 & =2s+36-36 \\\\ -16 & =2s \\\\ \\frac{-16}{2} & =\\frac{2s}{2} \\\\ -8 & =s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $s=-8$ is different from $s=-3$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $s=-8.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186083775 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186083775 1071 "Solve for $z$ where $\\frac{z}{3z - 9} = \\frac{2}{3}$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186083775 1071 "Solve for $z$ where $\\frac{z}{3z-9}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186083775 1074 "First, note that the denominator $3z-9$ cannot be zero, since division by $0$ is undefined. Therefore we cannot have $3z-9=0,$ which means $z=3$ cannot be a solution.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z}{3z-9} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ z\\cdot 3 & =(3z-9)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3z & =6z-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z & =6z-18 \\\\ 3z-6z & =6z-18-6z \\\\ -3z & =-18 \\\\ \\frac{-3z}{-3} & =\\frac{-18}{-3} \\\\ z & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $z=6$ is different from $z=3$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $z=6.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186084516 1064 "Let's consider the rational equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{x}=\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nWhen the equation consists of a single rational expression on each side, like the one above, then the easiest way to solve it is usually to use cross-multiplication.\n\nBefore we start, though, we note that $x=0$ *cannot* be a solution. This is because when $x=0$, we get a zero in the denominator on the left-hand side.\n\nNow, let's solve the equation. First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{x} &= \\frac{3}{5} \\\\\n2 \\cdot 5 &= x \\cdot 3 \\\\\n10 &= 3x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can solve for $x$ using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 &= 3x \\\\\n\\frac{10}{3} &= \\frac{3x}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{10}{3} &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nx= \\dfrac{10}{3}\n$$\n\nis different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{10}{3}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186084516 1064 "Let's consider the rational equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{x}=\\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhen the equation consists of a single rational expression on each side, like the one above, then the easiest way to solve it is usually to use cross-multiplication.\n\nBefore we start, though, we note that $x=0$ *cannot* be a solution. This is because when $x=0$, we get a zero in the denominator on the left-hand side.\n\nNow, let's solve the equation. First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 2\\cdot 5 & =x\\cdot 3 \\\\ 10 & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can solve for $x$ using the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =3x \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =\\frac{3x}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x= \\dfrac{10}{3}$ is different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x = \\dfrac{10}{3}.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186084517 1064 "If there is a negative sign in a rational equation, we can still solve it using cross-multiplication. The only difference is that we have to move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction first.\n\nFor example, let's solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = -\\dfrac 9 8\n$$\n\nThis is similar to the equation from the previous example, but this time, there is a negative sign on the right-hand side.\n\nWe deal with the negative sign by moving it onto the numerator of the fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed to solve the equation by cross-multiplication, as usual. (We also note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.) Cross-multiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac{3}{2x} &= \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8} \\\\[5pt] 3 \\cdot 8 &= 2x \\cdot (-9) \\\\[5pt] 24 &= -18x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 24 &= -18x \\\\[5pt] \\dfrac{24}{-18} &= \\dfrac{-18x}{-18} \\\\[5pt] -\\dfrac{4}{3} &= x \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nis different from $x=0$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186084517 1064 "If there is a negative sign in a rational equation, we can still solve it using cross-multiplication. The only difference is that we have to move the negative sign onto the numerator of the fraction first.\n\nFor example, let's solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = -\\dfrac 9 8 .\n$$\n\nThis is similar to the equation from the previous example, but this time, there is a negative sign on the right-hand side.\n\nWe deal with the negative sign by moving it onto the numerator of the fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2x} = \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8} .\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed to solve the equation by cross-multiplication, as usual. (We also note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.) Cross-multiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{3}{2x} &= \\dfrac{\\color{red}-9}{8} \\[5pt]\n\t\t3 \\cdot 8 &= 2x \\cdot (-9) \\[5pt]\n\t\t24 &= -18x\n\t}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t24 &= -18x \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{24}{-18} &= \\dfrac{-18x}{-18} \\[5pt]\n\t\t-\\dfrac{4}{3} &= x\n\t}\n$$\n\nSince  $x = 