^2\\right)^3y^0, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{\\left(2x^2\\right)^2 y^1}},\\qquad \\left(2x^2\\right)^1 y^2,\\qquad \\left(2x^2\\right)^0 y^3\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns $x^4y$ is the second. So, we are looking for the second term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,{\\color{red}3},3,1)$. Therefore, the second term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3(2x^{2})^{2}(y)^{1} &= 3(4x^{4})(y) \\\\\n&= 12x^{4}y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of $x^4y$ in the expansion of ${(2x^2+y)}^3$ is $12$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186100720 1074 "Since the exponent of the binomial expression is $n=3,$ we see that the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n\\left(2x^2\\right)^3y^0, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{\\left(2x^2\\right)^2 y^1}},\\qquad \\left(2x^2\\right)^1 y^2,\\qquad \\left(2x^2\\right)^0 y^3.\n$$\n\nOf these, the term that returns $x^4y$ is the second. So, we are looking for the second term in the binomial expansion.\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,{\\color{red}3},3,1).$ Therefore, the second term in the binomial expansion is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(2{x}^{2})}^{2}{(y)}^{1} & =3(4{x}^{4})(y) \\\\  & =12{x}^{4}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the coefficient of $x^4y$ in the expansion of ${(2x^2+y)}^3$ is $12.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186100805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492024 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186100805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492069 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186100806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492054 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186100807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492006 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186100807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492045 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186100807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492046 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186100808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491994 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186100808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492016 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186100808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492029 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186100808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492051 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101007 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492049 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101007 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492062 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101008 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492021 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101008 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492058 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101009 1038 #edb/ref 17592186491985 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101009 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492068 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101010 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492065 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101026 1050 900.0 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101117 1071 "Given that\n\n$(y + x^{2})^{3} = y^{3} + Ay^{2}x^{2} + Byx^{4} + Cx^{6}$\n\nwhere $A, B$ and $C$ are constants, determine the value of $A + B + C$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186101117 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n{(y+{x}^{2})}^{3}={y}^{3}+A{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+By{x}^{4}+C{x}^{6}\n$$\n\nwhere $A,B$ and $C$ are constants, determine the value of $A+B+C.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101117 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=y,$ and $b={x}^{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(y+{x}^{2})}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1(y)^{3}{({x}^{2})}^{0}+3(y)^{2}{({x}^{2})}^{1}+3(y)^{1}{({x}^{2})}^{2}+1(y)^{0}{({x}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =1{y}^{3}(1)+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+3y{x}^{4}+1(1){x}^{6} \\\\  & ={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}{x}^{2}+3y{x}^{4}+{x}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=3,B=3,$ and $C=1.$ Finally, then\n\n$$\nA+B+C=3+3+1=7.\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101400 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101400 1071 "Find, in ascending powers of $n$, the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2 - 5n)^{4}$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186101400 1071 "Find, in ascending powers of $n,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(2-5n)^{4}.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101400 1074 "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=2,$ and $b=-5n.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5n)^{4} & =1{a}^{4}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1(2)^{4}(-5n)^{0}+4(2)^{3}(-5n)^{1}+6(2)^{2}(-5n)^{2}+4(2)(-5n)^{3}+1(2)^{0}(-5n)^{4} \\\\  & =1(16)(1)+4(8)(-5n)+6(4)(25{n}^{2})+4(2)(-125{n}^{3})+1(1)(625{n}^{4}) \\\\  & =16-160n+600{n}^{2}-1\\,000{n}^{3}+625{n}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in ascending powers of $n$, the first three terms are\n\n$$\n16-160n+600{n}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101522 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the points $(0, 10)$ and $(4, 26)$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186101522 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the points $(0,10)$ and $(4,26).$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101522 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{26-10}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{4} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,b).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,10),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b=10.$ \n\nSubstituting the slope $m=4$ and the $y$-intercept $b=10$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=4x+10.\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101975 1064 "Multiplying a binomial by itself $n$ times is known as a **binomial expansion**.\nFor instance, multiplying the binomial $(a + b)$ by itself gives\n\n$$\n(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2\n$$\n\nLet's consider the binomial expansion of\n\n$$\n(a+b)^n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a positive integer.\n\nTo write out the expansion, we use two patterns to simplify most of the hard work: the **exponent pattern** and the **coefficient pattern**.\n\n- The **exponent pattern** is\n$a^{\\color{red}n}b^{\\color{blue}0},\\quad a^{\\color{red}{n-1}}b^{{\\color{blue}1}}, \\quad a^{{\\color{red}n-2}}b^{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad \\dots\\quad a^{\\color{red}2}b^{{\\color{blue}n-2}},\\quad a^{\\color{red}1}b^{{\\color{blue}n - 1}}, \\quad a^{\\color{red}0}b^{{\\color{blue}n}}$.\nThe exponents of $a$ start at $n$ and go down ($n, n-1, \\cdots, 1, 0$) while the exponents of $b$ increase from $0$ to $n$. In every term, the two exponents always add up to $n$.\n- The **coefficient pattern** can be found using Pascal's Triangle:\n$1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}10\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 5 & 1 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮\\begin{vmatrix}⋮ & ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮$\nWe take the top row to be the $0$th row. Then, the $n$th row of Pascal's Triangle are the coefficients of $(a+b)^n$.\n\nLet's use this to expand the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^3\n$$\n\nThe exponent pattern is\n\n$$\na^{\\color{red}3}b^{\\color{blue}0},\\quad a^{\\color{red}2}b^{\\color{blue}1}, \\quad a^{\\color{red}1}b^{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad a^{\\color{red}0}b^{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nSince $n=3$, the coefficient pattern is found using the $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle: $(1,3,3,1)$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(a + b)^3 = \\mathbf{1}a^{\\color{red}3}b^{\\color{blue}0} + \\mathbf{3}a^{\\color{red}2}b^{\\color{blue}1} + \\mathbf{3}a^{\\color{red}1}b^{\\color{blue}2} + \\mathbf{1}a^{\\color{red}0}b^{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying the above gives\n\n$$\n(a + b)^3= a^{3} + 3a^{2}b + 3ab^{2} + b^{3}\n$$\n\nand we're done!" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186101975 1064 "Multiplying a binomial by itself $n$ times is known as a **binomial expansion**.\nFor instance, multiplying the binomial $(a + b)$ by itself gives\n\n$$\n(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2.\n$$\n\nLet's consider the binomial expansion of \n\n$$\n(a+b)^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a positive integer.\n\nTo write out the expansion, we use two patterns to simplify most of the hard work: the **exponent pattern** and the **coefficient pattern**.\n\n- The **exponent pattern** is\n \n\n$$\na^{\\color{red}n}b^{\\color{blue}0},\\quad  a^{\\color{red}{n-1}}b^{{\\color{blue}1}}, \\quad   a^{{\\color{red}n-2}}b^{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad  \\dots\\quad   a^{\\color{red}2}b^{{\\color{blue}n-2}},\\quad    a^{\\color{red}1}b^{{\\color{blue}n - 1}}, \\quad   a^{\\color{red}0}b^{{\\color{blue}n}}.\n$$\n\nThe exponents of $a$ start at $n$ and go down ($n, n-1, \\cdots, 1, 0$) while the exponents of $b$ increase from $0$ to $n.$ In every term, the two exponents always add up to $n.$\n\n- The **coefficient pattern** can be found using Pascal's Triangle:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe take the top row to be the $0$th row. Then, the $n$th row of Pascal's Triangle are the coefficients of $(a+b)^n.$\n\nLet's use this to expand the binomial \n\n$$\n(a+b)^3.\n$$\n\nThe exponent pattern is\n\n$$\na^{\\color{red}3}b^{\\color{blue}0},\\quad  a^{\\color{red}2}b^{\\color{blue}1}, \\quad a^{\\color{red}1}b^{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad  a^{\\color{red}0}b^{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\nSince $n=3,$ the coefficient pattern is found using the $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle: $(1,3,3,1).$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(a + b)^3  \n= \\mathbf{1}a^{\\color{red}3}b^{\\color{blue}0} + \\mathbf{3}a^{\\color{red}2}b^{\\color{blue}1} + \\mathbf{3}a^{\\color{red}1}b^{\\color{blue}2} + \\mathbf{1}a^{\\color{red}0}b^{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying the above gives\n\n$$\n(a + b)^3= a^{3} + 3a^{2}b + 3ab^{2} + b^{3} ,\n$$\n\nand we're done!" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101977 1064 "One interesting number pattern is **Pascal's triangle**, named after Blaise Pascal, a famous French mathematician.\n\nPascal's triangle is made by rows of numbers that form a triangle.\nEach row has one more number than the row above.\nThe first two rows are made of three $1$s in a triangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's construct the next row.\nThe first and last number in each row is always $1.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & ◻ &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other numbers are always greater than $1$ and can be found by adding the two closest numbers located in the previous row.\n\nIn our case, the numbers closest to the unknown number and located in the previous row are ${\\color{blue}1}$ and ${\\color{blue}1}.$\nTherefore, the unknown number is the sum of $\\color{blue}1$ and ${\\color{blue}1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\large\\square} = {\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}1} = 2\n$$\n\nNow, we have the first three rows of Pascal's triangle.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we continue repeating this process for each row, we will get the following results for the first six rows of Pascal's triangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186101977 1064 "One interesting number pattern is **Pascal's triangle**, named after Blaise Pascal, a famous French mathematician.\n\nPascal's triangle is made by rows of numbers that form a triangle.\nEach row has one more number than the row above.\nThe first two rows are made of three $1$s in a triangle:\n$1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮ \\mid ⋮$\n\nLet's construct the next row.\nThe first and last number in each row is always $1$.\n$1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}◻\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮ \\mid ⋮ \\mid ⋮$\n\nThe other numbers are always greater than $1$ and can be found by adding the two closest numbers located in the previous row.\n\nIn our case, the numbers closest to the unknown number and located in the previous row are ${\\color{blue}1}$ and ${\\color{blue}1}$.\nTherefore, the unknown number is the sum of $\\color{blue}1$ and ${\\color{blue}1}$:\n\n$$\n{\\large\\square} = {\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}1} = 2\n$$\n\nNow, we have the first three rows of Pascal's triangle.\n$1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ ⋮\\end{vmatrix}⋮ \\mid ⋮ \\mid ⋮$\n\nIf we continue repeating this process for each row, we will get the following results for the first six rows of Pascal's triangle:\n$1; 1\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}2\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}3\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}6\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}10\\