7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of $\\triangle ABD$ is $p=17.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186105529 1071 "In a pentagon $ABCDE$, the measures of the sides are ${AB} = 25\\,\\mathrm{mm}$, ${BC} = 26\\,\\mathrm{mm}$, ${CD} = 30\\,\\mathrm{mm}$, ${DE} = 18\\,\\mathrm{mm}$, and ${EA} = 21\\,\\mathrm{mm}$.\nWhat is the perimeter of the pentagon?" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186105529 1071 "In a pentagon $ABCDE$, the measures of the sides are ${AB} = 25\\,\\mathrm{mm},$ ${BC} = 26\\,\\mathrm{mm},$ ${CD} = 30\\,\\mathrm{mm},$ ${DE} = 18\\,\\mathrm{mm},$ and ${EA} = 21\\,\\mathrm{mm}.$\nWhat is the perimeter of the pentagon?" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186105529 1074 "The perimeter $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. So, we can simply calculate\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= 25mm + 26mm + 30mm + 18mm + 21mm \\\\\n&= 120mm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is a large quantity of $\\textrm{mm}$, we will convert to $\\textrm{cm}$:\n\n$$\np = 120\\, \\mathrm{mm} = 12\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186105529 1074 "The perimeter $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. So, we can simply calculate\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =25\\,mm+26\\,mm+30\\,mm+18\\,mm+21\\,mm \\\\  & =120\\,mm\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is a large quantity of $\\textrm{mm},$ we will convert to $\\textrm{cm}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\np = 120\\, \\mathrm{mm} = 12\\,\\mathrm{cm}.\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186105530 1071 "A regular pentagon has the same perimeter as an equilateral triangle whose side is $15\\,\\mathrm{cm}$. Find the length of the side of the pentagon." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186105530 1071 "A regular pentagon has the same perimeter as an equilateral triangle whose side is $15\\,\\mathrm{cm}.$ Find the length of the side of the pentagon." 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186105530 1074 "Since all the sides of an equilateral triangle are equal, its perimeter is\n\n$$\np=3\\cdot 15=45\n$$\n\nLet $a$ be the side of the pentagon. We know that our polygons have equal perimeters. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= 5a \\\\\n45 &= 5a \\\\\n9 &= a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of the side is $9 \\,\\mathrm{cm}$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186105530 1074 "Since all the sides of an equilateral triangle are equal, its perimeter is \n\n$$\np=3\\cdot 15=45.\n$$\n\nLet $a$ be the side of the pentagon. We know that our polygons have equal perimeters. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =5a \\\\ 45 & =5a \\\\ 9 & =a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of the side is $9 \\,\\mathrm{cm}.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186105806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492074 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186105807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492033 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186105807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492041 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186105808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492007 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186105808 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492071 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186105897 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492050 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186105898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186492072 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106009 1050 540.0 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106066 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106066 1071 "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $PQ^(―)$, where $P(1, 0, 3)$ and $Q(1, 2, 0)$.\n$r(t) = i + aj + bk, t ∈ [c, 1]$\nWhat is $a + b + c$?" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186106066 1071 "The equation below gives a parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(1,0,3)$ and $Q(1,2,0).$\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+a\\,j+b\\,k,\\,t\\in [c,1]\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b+c?$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106066 1074 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)-(i+3\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\,j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\nr=p+tv\\,⟹\\,r=(i+3\\,k)+t(2\\,j-3\\,k)\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(i+3\\,k)+t(2\\,j-3\\,k) \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+(3-3t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=2t+(3-3t)+0=3-t.\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106137 1071 "![](images/4a/4ab3c4ba281be5363c5ea3e2b3fce6aec91221b31a762ef645c7262095866d5e.png)\n\nThe perimeter $p_{1}$ of the triangle $△DBC$ shown above is $10$. What is the perimeter of the triangle $△ABC$ if $BD = 3$?" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186106137 1071 "![](images/4a/4ab3c4ba281be5363c5ea3e2b3fce6aec91221b31a762ef645c7262095866d5e.png)\n\nThe perimeter ${p}_{1}$ of the triangle $△DBC$ shown above is $10.$ What is the perimeter of the triangle $△ABC$ if $BD=3?$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106137 1074 "From the diagram, we can see that $△ABC$ is an equilateral triangle and $△DBC$ is an isosceles triangle with $CD=DB.$\n\nWe're told that the perimeter of $△DBC$ is ${p}_{1}=10,$ and $DB=3,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}_{1} & =DB+BC+CD \\\\ 10 & =3+BC+3 \\\\ 4 & =BC.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo now we have enough information to compute the perimeter of the triangle $△ABC:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =3BC \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of $△ABC$ is $p=12.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106283 1071 "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(-2, 5,-3)$ and is parallel to $v = ⟨1,-1, 2⟩$. Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP = \\sqrt{24}$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186106283 1071 "Consider the straight line that passes through $P(-2,5,-3)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$ Find all the possible points $A$ on that line such that $AP=\\sqrt{24}.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106283 1074 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩)-⟨-2,5,-3⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,-t,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{24},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,-t,\\,2t⟩| & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+(-t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\sqrt{6{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{6} & =2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨-2,5,-3⟩+2⟨1,-1,2⟩=⟨0,3,1⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨-2,5,-3⟩-2⟨1,-1,2⟩=⟨-4,7,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(0,3,1)$ and ${A}_{2}(-4,7,-7).$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106737 1061 228209 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186106737 1061 623181 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106738 1061 228210 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186106738 1061 623182 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106739 1061 228213 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186106739 1061 623185 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106740 1061 228214 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186106740 1061 623186 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106741 1061 228218 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186106741 1061 623189 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106742 1061 228219 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186106742 1061 623190 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186107044 1064 "Consider the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46a85010d46969ffb7df440efd72bb3fa8d1394b35cc2887ee4567cda15079c5.png)\n\nThe sum of the length of its sides is called the **perimeter**. Here, the perimeter of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AB + BC + AC \\\\\n&= 2 + 4.2 + 3.8 \\\\\n&= 10cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186107044 1064 "Consider the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46a85010d46969ffb7df440efd72bb3fa8d1394b35cc2887ee4567cda15079c5.png)\n\nThe sum of the length of its sides is called the **perimeter**. Here, the perimeter of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+AC \\\\  & =2+4.2+3.8 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186107045 1064 "Suppose we have a quadrilateral $ABCD$ as shown below and it is known that\n\n$$\n{AD} = 6\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\n$$\n{AB} = 2\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\n$$\n{BC} = 4\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nCD=5\\,\\mathrm{m}\n$$\n\nWhat is the perimeter of the quadrilateral?\n\n![](images/a7/a74251f1c72eccad42b98e0d3fd9a7e7cd0a60018a6f03bb65ec2abc34eb1c88.png)\n\nRemember that the **perimeter** $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. So, we can simply calculate\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AD + AB + BC + CD \\\\\n&= 6 + 2 + 4 + 5 \\\\\n&= 17m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186107045 1064 "Suppose we have a quadrilateral $ABCD$ as shown below and it is known that ${AD} = 6\\,\\mathrm{m},$ ${AB} = 2\\,\\mathrm{m},$ ${BC} = 4\\,\\mathrm{m},$ and $CD=5\\,\\mathrm{m}.$ What is the perimeter of the quadrilateral?   \n\n![](images/a7/a74251f1c72eccad42b98e0d3fd9a7e7cd0a60018a6f03bb65ec2abc34eb1c88.png)\n\nRemember that the **perimeter** $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. So, we can simply calculate \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AD+AB+BC+CD \\\\  & =6+2+4+5 \\\\  & =17\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186107046 1064 "Let's take a look at the polygons depicted below and find their perimeters.\n\n![](images/69/69ef06a71f13625ad4525a03966f4a4c62ae2b2136b085e97b8dc02a6a6963cc.png)\n\nFrom the picture, we conclude that polygons I, II and III all have a perimeter of $10$ while polygon IV has a perimeter of $8$.\n\nPolygons that have the same perimeter are called **isoperimetric**. So I, II and III are isoperimetric polygons." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186107046 1064 "Let's take a look at the polygons depicted below and find their perimeters.\n\n![](images/69/69ef06a71f13625ad4525a03966f4a4c62ae2b2136b085e97b8dc02a6a6963cc.png)\n\nFrom the picture, we conclude that polygons I, II and III all have a perimeter of $10$ while polygon IV has a perimeter of $8.$ \n\nPolygons that have the same perimeter are called **isoperimetric**. So I, II and III are isoperimetric polygons." 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186110326 1071 "Find the solution to $\\dfrac {1} {x-2} = 5 .$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186110326 1071 "Find the solution to $\\dfrac {1} {x-2} = 5$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186110326 1074 "First, note that $x=2$ cannot be a solution. This is because if $x=2$, then the rational term would be\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{0}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined because it has a zero denominator.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(x-2)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{x - 2} &= 5 \\\\\n(x - 2) \\cdot \\frac{1}{x - 2} &= (x - 2) \\cdot 5 \\\\\n(x - 2) \\cdot \\frac{1}{x - 2} &= 5x - 10 \\\\\n1 &= 5x - 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 5x - 10 \\\\\n1 + 10 &= 5x - 10 + 10 \\\\\n11 &= 5x \\\\\n\\frac{11}{5} &= \\frac{5x}{5} \\\\\n\\frac{11}{5} &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{11}{5}\n$$\n\nis different from $x=2$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{11}{5}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186110326 1074 "First, note that $x=2$ cannot be a solution. This is because if $x=2,$ then the rational term would be $\\dfrac{1}{0},$ which is undefined because it has a zero denominator.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(x-2)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-2} & =5 \\\\ (x-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2} & =(x-2)\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{(x-2)}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{x-2}} & =5x-10 \\\\ 1 & =5x-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =5x-10 \\\\ 1+10 & =5x-10+10 \\\\ 11 & =5x \\\\ \\frac{11}{5} & =\\frac{5x}{5} \\\\ \\frac{11}{5} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x=\\dfrac{11}{5}$ is different from $x=2$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=\\dfrac{11}{5}.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186110327 1071 "Solve $\\dfrac {3x} {x+1} = 2 .$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186110327 1071 "Solve $\\dfrac {3x} {x+1} = 2$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186110327 1074 "First, note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(x+1)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3x}{x + 1} &= 2 \\\\\n(x + 1) \\cdot \\frac{3x}{x + 1} &= (x + 1) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n(x + 1) \\cdot \\frac{3x}{x + 1} &= 2x + 2 \\\\\n3x &= 2x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x &= 2x + 2 \\\\\n3x - 2x &= 2x + 2 - 2x \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x = 2$ is different from $x=-1$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=2$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186110327 1074 "First, note that $x=-1$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(x+1)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{x+1} & =2 \\\\ (x+1)\\cdot \\frac{3x}{x+1} & =(x+1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{(x+1)}\\cdot \\frac{3x}{\\cancel{x+1}} & =2x+2 \\\\ 3x & =2x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =2x+2 \\\\ 3x-2x & =2x+2-2x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x = 2$ is different from $x=-1$, we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $x=2.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186110328 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac {1} {2x+1} = 2 .$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186110328 1071 "Solve the equation $\\dfrac {1} {2x+1} = 2$." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186110328 1074 "First, note that the denominator $2x+1$ cannot be zero, since division by $0$ is undefined. Therefore we cannot have\n\n$$\n2x+1=0\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\ncannot be a solution.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first multiply both sides by the denominator $(2x+1)$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2x + 1} &= 2 \\\\\n(2x + 1) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2x + 1} &= (2x + 1) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n(2x + 1) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2x + 1} &= 4x + 2 \\\\\n1 &= 4x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 4x + 2 \\\\\n1 - 2 &= 4x + 2 - 2 \\\\\n- 1 &= 4x \\\\\n\\frac{-1}{4} &= \\frac{4x}{4} \\\\\n- \\frac{1}{4} &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nis different from\n\n$$\nx