_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})^{2}-3({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}){y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}-3{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+6{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}^{2}-7{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+11{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186163227 1064 "Suppose we are given the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= x_1^2-8x_1x_2 + x_2^2\n$$\n\n and the matrix ![](images/13/13fad01fd9fcf327f8c45afa8264d92d9de1db135b7a87add4e43b9617574d8d.svg) that defines  the **change of variable**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ![](images/c6/c6f0e1bf4282bf2ffedc462005ea30ad8bbe07917bab42eaf7fedf8fd2c49ef7.svg) and ![](images/ad/ad7dec0dac6e2a50d428c7d7dbeda4e71812df2f6ac2d80d6d834ffc82f4a0cb.svg) \n\n How do we express $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ in terms of the components of $\\mathbf{y}?$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n![](images/ae/aefd4438342fcc5201fd2a3a04e4419f9c5189c4cd103eb63fa1ab6d464edc35.svg) \n\ngives the following expressions for $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we substitute these into our quadratic form expression, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})^{2}+8(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}){y}_{2}+(-{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & =4{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+16{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-8{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4{y}_{1}^{2}+12{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186163227 1064 "Suppose we are given the quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= x_1^2-8x_1x_2 + x_2^2\n$$\n\n and the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ that defines  the **change of variable**\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y} ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ \n\n How do we express $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ in terms of the components of $\\mathbf{y}?$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\ngives the following expressions for $x_1$ and $x_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we substitute these into our quadratic form expression, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}-8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})^{2}+8(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}){y}_{2}+(-{y}_{2})^{2} \\\\  & =4{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{1}{y}_{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+16{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-8{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4{y}_{1}^{2}+12{y}_{1}{y}_{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186163230 1064 "In certain applications involving quadratic forms, an important question is whether a particular quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)$ is *always* positive or *always* negative.\n\nWe classify quadratic forms using the following terminology:\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **positive-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) > 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nFor example, the quadratic form\n\n$$\nS(\\mathbf{x})=8x_1^2+3x_2^2\n$$\n\nis positive-definite since\n$8x_1^2+3x_2^2 > 0$\nfor all nonzero $\\mathbf{x}.$\n\n![](images/52/52e43ebb6fa34f40a73358f88aed62846332126c4fe080072c211945f54b412a.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **negative-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) <     0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nFor example, the quadratic form \n\n$$\nT(\\mathbf{x})=-x_1^2-3x_2^2\n$$\n\nis negative-definite since\n$-x_1^2-3x_2^2 \\lt 0$\nfor all nonzero $\\mathbf{x}.$\n\n![](images/a8/a86f80966c04f960823db91bfc8711eef2bf22a81502e69b5048afc5b4799cb2.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **indefinite** if \n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x}_1) > 0$ for some $\\mathbf{x}_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n,$ and\n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x}_2) <     0$ for some $\\mathbf{x}_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the form\n\n$$\nR(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+2x_2^2-6x_1x_2\n$$\n\nis indefinite. Substituting ![](images/09/094a61c791563994cb520b40179d77546f776bfb9148851e211ec8ed326c279a.svg) we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{1}) & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot {0}^{2}-6\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0-0 \\\\  & =1>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting ![](images/f4/f4995691f0b24159baeb3cbb1ee51a9f44c72958c9288809f28f387d17b2810d.svg) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{2}) & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot {1}^{2}-6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+2-6 \\\\  & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186163230 1064 "In certain applications involving quadratic forms, an important question is whether a particular quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x)$ is *always* positive or *always* negative.\n\nWe classify quadratic forms using the following terminology:\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **positive-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) > 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nFor example, the quadratic form\n\n$$\nS(\\mathbf{x})=8x_1^2+3x_2^2\n$$\n\nis positive-definite since\n$8x_1^2+3x_2^2 > 0$\nfor all nonzero $\\mathbf{x}.$\n\n![](images/52/52e43ebb6fa34f40a73358f88aed62846332126c4fe080072c211945f54b412a.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **negative-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) <     0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n \\setminus \\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\nFor example, the quadratic form \n\n$$\nT(\\mathbf{x})=-x_1^2-3x_2^2\n$$\n\nis negative-definite since\n$-x_1^2-3x_2^2 \\lt 0$\nfor all nonzero $\\mathbf{x}.$\n\n![](images/a8/a86f80966c04f960823db91bfc8711eef2bf22a81502e69b5048afc5b4799cb2.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **indefinite** if \n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x}_1) > 0$ for some $\\mathbf{x}_1 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n,$ and\n\n- $Q(\\mathbf{x}_2) <     0$ for some $\\mathbf{x}_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the form\n\n$$\nR(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+2x_2^2-6x_1x_2\n$$\n\nis indefinite. Substituting $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{1}) & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot {0}^{2}-6\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1+0-0 \\\\  & =1>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R({x}_{2}) & ={1}^{2}+2\\cdot {1}^{2}-6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+2-6 \\\\  & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186163236 1064 "Suppose we are given a quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\n \n\nand an invertible matrix $P$ that defines  the change of variable\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIf this change of variable is applied to the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ what will be the new matrix of $Q$?\n\nBy making the necessary substitutions, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}{P}^{T}\\,AP\\,y \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}AP)y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the new matrix of our quadratic form is \n\n$$\nB=  P^T \\! A P.\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186163236 1064 "Suppose we are given a quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})= \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A\\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\n \n\nand an invertible matrix $P$ that defines  the change of variable\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} =P \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIf this change of variable is applied to the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ what will be the new matrix of $Q$?\n\nBy making the necessary substitutions, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}^{T}\\,Ax \\\\  & =(Py)^{T}\\,A\\,(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}{P}^{T}\\,AP\\,y \\\\  & ={y}^{T}({P}^{T}AP)y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the new matrix of our quadratic form is \n\n$$\nB=  P^T \\! A P.\n$$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186163237 1064 "Two quadratic forms $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ on $\\Bbb R^n$ are said to be **equivalent** if they can be obtained from each other through a change of variable.\n\n*The two quadratic forms with matrices $A$ and $B$ are equivalent if and only if there exists an invertible matrix $P$ such that* \n\n$$\nB = P^T \\! A \\, P.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Equivalent quadratic forms represent the same function from  $\\Bbb R^n$ to $\\Bbb R.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186163237 1064 "Two quadratic forms $Q_1$ and $Q_2$ on $\\Bbb R^n$ are said to be **equivalent** if they can be obtained from each other through a change of variable.\n\n> *The two quadratic forms with matrices $A$ and $B$ are equivalent if and only if there exists an invertible matrix $P$ such that* \n> \n> $$\n> B = P^T \\! A \\, P.\n> $$\n\n**Note:** Equivalent quadratic forms represent the same function from  $\\Bbb R^n$ to $\\Bbb R.$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186163241 1064 "Given a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}$ with a symmetric matrix $A$,  we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive,\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nUsing the same criteria, we can also classify the symmetric matrix $A$ as positive-definite, positive semi-definite, etc.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/85/8582c8b840b1e4696b9f83cb2c117d67e3b832e40b1ded716e0dcd23752c6f9c.svg)\n\nComputing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +24 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +6) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4,\\,-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative, the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) =  \\mathbf{x}^T A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is negative-definite." 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186163241 1064 "Given a quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}$ with a symmetric matrix $A$,  we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive,\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nUsing the same criteria, we can also classify the symmetric matrix $A$ as positive-definite, positive semi-definite, etc.\n\nFor example, consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nComputing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +24 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda +6) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4,\\,-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative, the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf x) =  \\mathbf{x}^T A \\, \\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is negative-definite." 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186163318 1064 "There are two further classifications of quadratic forms that turn out to be useful:\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **positive semi-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the form $A(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2$ over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is positive semi-definite. Notice that this particular form is not positive-definite since it gives zero on the nonzero vector ![](images/d3/d32277694c7bd75cf76ea413fba14b25a679f84e8194c07af201e103d23a1056.svg) \n\n![](images/8d/8d0adf62e90acc2f5a2a3fdbad2b87fc53e4f50145e38d8120cd932282afbb42.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **negative semi-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$ \n\nFor example, the form $B(\\mathbf{x})=-x_1^2$ over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is negative semi-definite. Notice that this particular form is not negative-definite since it gives zero on the nonzero vector ![](images/05/0592bc60943ac605d21672288a437282e3dddd52384b518f8869645f05540662.svg) \n\n![](images/76/76feacaad709f2f3a3c1dac8c0ee88aa461aed827270d0127f61de38d95489b6.png)\n\nIt's also worth noting that\n\n- any positive-definite quadratic form is positive semi-definite, and\n\n- any negative-definite quadratic form is negative semi-definite." 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186163318 1064 "There are two further classifications of quadratic forms that turn out to be useful:\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **positive semi-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the form $A(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2$ over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is positive semi-definite. Notice that this particular form is not positive-definite since it gives zero on the nonzero vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ \n\n![](images/8d/8d0adf62e90acc2f5a2a3fdbad2b87fc53e4f50145e38d8120cd932282afbb42.png)\n\n- A quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ is **negative semi-definite** if $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq 0$ for all $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$ \n\nFor example, the form $B(\\mathbf{x})=-x_1^2$ over $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is negative semi-definite. Notice that this particular form is not negative-definite since it gives zero on the nonzero vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ \n\n![](images/76/76feacaad709f2f3a3c1dac8c0ee88aa461aed827270d0127f61de38d95489b6.png)\n\nIt's also worth noting that\n\n- any positive-definite quadratic fo