{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times Z\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in Z\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $-1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(x,-1)\\,:\\,x\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/8e/8e677c814756c4818c25da450d789c974787df60cf4bb18017a96593d3ecc180.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f9/f99beeb00c070048ee4ee7c1b6c9e077d7d4e0f3fd9e63aa12b5b58588e14dbb.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times Z & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times Z$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/89/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png)" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186287752 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times Z\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is an integer number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in Z}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $-1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(x,-1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/8e/8e677c814756c4818c25da450d789c974787df60cf4bb18017a96593d3ecc180.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f9/f99beeb00c070048ee4ee7c1b6c9e077d7d4e0f3fd9e63aa12b5b58588e14dbb.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times Z & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times Z$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/89/89ac9e71d8152097657f0c01daecf2b894dc142db4ba27fbfadcb8ded6e9cb19.png)" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186287875 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,2]\\times [-0.5,0]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,2],b\\in [-0.5,0]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(2,0),$ $(2,-0.5),$ and $(0,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/58/5837864bfcc40e680cf62b43c0e3d145ba05b1f25e1ccae2f4788902d030d927.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,2]\\times [-i,0]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,2],b\\in [-i,0]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(2,0),$ $(2,-i),$ and $(0,-i).$\n\n![](images/f0/f0c20436f03db4b0003d087827cefbd76506af423da3f0a3042082b5afeb4b58.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[0,2]\\times \\{0\\}:$\n\n![](images/23/236d10f31265b400036e1ddeafa122991bcae1ea878af8b300003fb11acfa85b.png)" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186287875 1074 "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=0.5,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{0.5}=[0,2]\\times [-0.5,0]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,2],b\\in [-0.5,0]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(2,0),$ $(2,-0.5),$ and $(0,-0.5)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/58/5837864bfcc40e680cf62b43c0e3d145ba05b1f25e1ccae2f4788902d030d927.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[0,2]\\times [-i,0]={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [0,2],b\\in [-i,0]}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(0,0),$ $(2,0),$ $(2,-i),$ and $(0,-i).$\n\n![](images/f0/f0c20436f03db4b0003d087827cefbd76506af423da3f0a3042082b5afeb4b58.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $[0,2]\\times {0}:$\n\n![](images/23/236d10f31265b400036e1ddeafa122991bcae1ea878af8b300003fb11acfa85b.png)" 13194139535291 17592186045417 false] [17592186371872 1074 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y=2,\\,{x}^{2}y=4,\\,xy=1,\\,xy=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}y,\\,v=xy\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{2}y=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & {x}^{2}y=4\\,⟹\\,u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & xy=3\\,⟹\\,v=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/ba/baa8e8ab6cff8edec84d3f0d0950931f247153523e3320abbdf673161afb44ae.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ y & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\fr